 ## Overview
-TopoGS is a topology-aware anchor feature aggregation framework designed to address the feature isolation problem in octree-based 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) representations [S1, unofficial]. It solves the issue where anchor features at different octree levels are optimized in isolation, leading to high redundancy and poor cross-level information flow [S1, unofficial]. Unlike standard uniform mean pooling which treats all adjacent-level anchors equally, TopoGS distinguishes anchors participating in valid parent/child containment relations from isolated anchors, establishing bidirectional cross-level gradient pathways [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Octree-based Splatting](8067-octree-based-splatting.md) by explicitly modeling the octree's topological structure during feature aggregation rather than relying solely on spatial proximity or error metrics for primitive selection [S1, unofficial].
+TopoGS is a topology-aware anchor feature aggregation framework designed to address the feature isolation problem in octree-based 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) representations [S1, unofficial]. It solves the issue where anchor features at different octree levels are optimized in isolation, leading to high cross-level similarity and poor information flow [S1, unofficial]. Unlike standard uniform mean pooling which treats all adjacent-level anchors equally, TopoGS distinguishes anchors participating in valid parent/child containment relations from isolated anchors, establishing bidirectional cross-level gradient pathways [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Octree-based Splatting](8067-octree-based-splatting.md) by explicitly modeling the octree's topological structure during feature aggregation rather than relying solely on spatial proximity or error metrics for primitive selection [S1, unofficial].
 
